-## Downstream Contract Work
+An unsuccessful validation, idempotency, or stale-resolution request creates no revision and changes no state.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Submission Contract
+
+The submission envelope contains `visitId`, `baseRevisionId`, complete `payload`, and `submissionKey`. Omitting `baseRevisionId` or setting it to `null` denotes an initial submission; it is not a way to bypass conflict detection.
+
+The server authenticates the worker, validates the envelope and scientific fields, and resolves the catalog plot UUID before creating a revision. A non-null base must belong to the same visit. A missing or unrelated base is invalid rather than being treated as an edit against current state.
+
+A successful response returns the stored `revisionId`, its `baseRevisionId`, `receivedAt`, and whether the revision became current or entered the review set. This acceptance result describes that submission's transaction, not a promise that the revision remains current after later activity.
+
+Retries use the same complete envelope and `submissionKey`. Envelopes are compared after JSON Canonicalization Scheme (RFC 8785) canonicalization, so object-key order, whitespace, and equivalent JSON number representations do not create conflicts. An equivalent envelope from the originally authenticated worker returns the original acceptance result. Reuse of the key with a different envelope or by a different worker is rejected as an idempotency conflict. Key comparison is global so accidental reuse across visits cannot return another visit's result.
+
+Only a successfully accepted submission reserves its key. Parsing and canonicalization happen before key lookup; an existing key is then checked for an exact worker-and-envelope match before current domain validation, allowing a legitimate retry to succeed even if the catalog has since changed. For a new key, domain validation and the atomic creation of both the key reservation and revision must succeed together. A global uniqueness constraint resolves same-key races across different visits independently of per-visit serialization; the first accepted envelope wins and all others receive the replay or conflict result.
+
+## Retrieval Contract
+
+Current-state retrieval returns a single consistent snapshot containing:
+
+- the visit ID and current complete revision;
+- an `unresolved` indicator;
+- when unresolved, every revision in the outstanding review set in receipt order, including complete payloads, base IDs, authors, and receipt times.
+
+Submission-history retrieval returns all revisions and resolution records in a per-visit sequence assigned by the committed transaction order. Sequence numbers, rather than timestamps, define ordering and break timestamp ties. Revisions and resolutions retain their own receipt or resolution timestamps. Retrieving a visit never resolves or otherwise mutates its review state.
+
+## Resolution Contract
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit by supplying the visit ID, the selected revision ID from the current outstanding review set, and the complete set of revision IDs the coordinator compared. Expected review sets are unordered, duplicate-free collections. The selected complete payload becomes current without modification. No new scientific revision is created, and non-winning submissions remain in history.
+
+The expected-set comparison makes review decisions optimistic-concurrency operations. If another revision joins the set before resolution commits, the set no longer matches and the resolution is rejected as stale. The coordinator must retrieve and compare the expanded set rather than unknowingly closing an unseen submission. A revision outside the outstanding set cannot be selected. Validation rejects duplicate expected IDs before set comparison; a stale set is reported before an invalid selected ID so the coordinator refreshes unseen work first.
+
+A successful resolution records the coordinator ID, server resolution time, selected revision ID, and every revision ID in the resolved set in receipt order. Resolution and read-set verification are atomic with respect to new submissions for that visit. The operation clears only that outstanding set; later edits use the selected revision as current and follow the ordinary acceptance rules.
+
+A resolution request is identified by its visit ID, selected revision ID, and expected set. An exact coordinator-and-request replay returns the original resolution record, including after the visit has become current. Concurrent identical requests have the same idempotent outcome. A request from another coordinator or with different inputs after resolution receives the applicable state or stale-set error rather than altering the recorded resolution.
+
+The resolution itself is immutable. Correcting an earlier choice requires a new visit submission based on current state, followed by review if another worker has advanced the visit independently.
+
+## API Shape
+
+| Operation | Inputs | Success result | Main rejection cases |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Submit revision | Visit ID, optional base revision ID, complete payload, submission key | Stored revision and current/review placement | Invalid payload or plot, unknown or unrelated base, key conflict |
+| Retrieve current visit | Visit ID | Current revision, unresolved indicator, outstanding review set | Unknown visit |
+| Retrieve visit history | Visit ID | All revisions and resolutions in receipt order | Unknown visit |
+| Resolve review set | Visit ID, selected revision ID, expected complete review set | Resolution record and selected current revision | Stale set, revision outside set, visit not unresolved |
+
+Authenticated workers can submit and retrieve visits. Only coordinators can resolve review sets. Client-visible errors carry stable machine-readable codes for unauthenticated or forbidden access, malformed input, invalid scientific fields or plot references, unknown visit or revision, unrelated base revision, idempotency conflict, visit not unresolved, duplicate expected revision IDs, stale review set, and selection outside the review set. Error payloads do not expose another user's submission payload.
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+Tablet-sync must persist a base revision ID with every editable visit and submit complete visit payloads with globally unique submission keys. It must retain the exact envelope for retries and treat current/review placement as a server outcome rather than assuming that a later submission wins. Its reconciliation flow must handle growing review sets and refresh workers' base revision IDs after a resolution becomes visible.
+
+Review-export must compare complete revisions rather than individual changed fields. It must keep unresolved visits out of the reviewed CSV even though they still have a readable current revision, refresh and resubmit comparisons when a stale review-set response arrives, and retain links from an exported current revision to its resolution provenance. Queue views must tolerate revisions arriving while a coordinator is comparing them.
+
+## Concurrency Alternatives
+
+Field-aware three-way merge remains a credible future direction if coordinator workload becomes the dominant operational cost. Disjoint field edits could reduce the number of visits awaiting review, and field-level provenance could expose small automatic combinations without whole-payload comparison. Adopting it would require tablet base snapshots and structured change envelopes instead of only base revision IDs, maintained merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries, plus field-level provenance in review and export. It would not remove the need to preserve original submissions or review overlapping edits.
+
+That reduction in review work carries the central scientific risk: an apparently disjoint combination can still cross related observations, such as a species identification and its cover estimate. Whole-visit revisions accept a larger review queue to keep that judgment with coordinators. Last-writer-wins is not an acceptable alternative because arrival order has no scientific authority and would silently discard edits.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit semantics | Immutable whole-visit revisions with coordinator selection | Field-aware three-way merge; last-writer-wins | Preserves each worker's scientific intent and avoids maintained merge rules combining related edits incorrectly, at the cost of a larger review queue. |
+| Representation of a resolution | Point current state at an existing complete revision and store a separate resolution record | Create a synthetic merged revision; rewrite the selected submission | Keeps submitted observations immutable and makes the coordinator's choice auditable without implying field-level composition. |
+| Submission while review is pending | Accept into the outstanding review set | Reject further edits; automatically close or replace earlier review work | Offline workers cannot coordinate before submitting, and every valid submitted edit must remain available for review. |
+| Concurrent transaction safety | Serialize submission placement and resolution per visit; verify the exact review set at resolution | Rely on arrival order; resolve against only a selected revision | Prevents unseen concurrent submissions from being displaced by either automatic advancement or coordinator resolution. |
+| Idempotency scope | Globally unique submission keys compared against the complete envelope | Per-visit keys; payload-only comparison | Prevents cross-visit key collisions and makes base revision and authorship metadata part of retry identity. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Whole-visit revisions are the concurrency policy; field-aware three-way merge is not used.
+2. Unresolved visits retain a readable current revision but cannot enter reviewed export.
+3. Resolutions select one complete submitted revision and do not synthesize field-level combinations.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Physical storage technology, indexes, retention limits, and operational backup procedures.
+2. Pagination and filtering for large submission histories and review queues.
+3. Tablet-sync presentation of review-set growth and review-export comparison layout.
+
