+## Submission and Retention
+
+- [ ] **REC-001**: When an authenticated worker submits a valid whole-visit revision, the system SHALL retain its complete payload, visit ID, base revision ID or initial-submission marker, author ID, server receipt time, server-generated revision ID, and globally unique submission key.
+- [ ] **REC-002**: When the originally authenticated worker repeats an accepted submission key with an RFC 8785 canonically equivalent envelope, the system SHALL return the original acceptance result without creating another revision.
+- [ ] **REC-003**: If a submission reuses an accepted submission key with a different canonical envelope or authenticated worker, then the system SHALL reject it with an idempotency-conflict error and leave the original revision unchanged.
+- [ ] **REC-004**: When revisions derived from the same starting visit state are accepted, the system SHALL preserve every complete original submission regardless of which revision is current or selected during resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-007**: When the first valid submission for an unknown visit ID is accepted, the system SHALL set that revision as the visit's current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-008**: When a valid edit is based on the current revision of a visit with no outstanding review set, the system SHALL set the new revision as current.
+- [ ] **REC-009**: When a valid edit has a non-current base, repeats an initial submission for an existing visit, or arrives while its visit has an outstanding review set, the system SHALL add the complete revision to that visit's outstanding review set without displacing the current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-010**: When processing submissions for the same visit, the system SHALL serialize revision placement with other submissions and resolutions for that visit so that concurrent requests cannot both advance the same current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-011**: If a non-null base revision does not exist or belongs to another visit, then the system SHALL reject the submission without changing the visit or reserving its submission key.
+- [ ] **REC-012**: If a submission fails parsing, canonicalization, authorization, or validation before acceptance, then the system SHALL create no revision, reserve no new submission key, and change no visit state.
+- [ ] **REC-013**: When multiple new submissions concurrently use one unused submission key, the system SHALL atomically admit at most one revision for that key across all visits.
+- [ ] **REC-014**: When a successful submission response is returned, the system SHALL include the stored revision ID, base revision ID, server receipt time, and whether the revision became current or entered the outstanding review set.
+
+## Current-State and History Retrieval
+
+- [ ] **REC-005**: When a caller retrieves a visit, the system SHALL return its current complete revision and an explicit indicator of whether an outstanding review set exists.
+- [ ] **REC-015**: While a visit has an outstanding review set, current-state retrieval SHALL return every revision in that set with its complete payload, base revision ID, author ID, and receipt time.
+- [ ] **REC-016**: While a visit has an outstanding review set, the system SHALL mark the visit ineligible for reviewed export despite retaining its readable current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-017**: When current-state retrieval overlaps a submission or resolution, the system SHALL return the current revision, unresolved indicator, and review-set revisions from one consistent snapshot.
+- [ ] **REC-018**: The system SHALL return each outstanding review set in revision receipt order.
+- [ ] **REC-019**: When a caller retrieves a visit's history, the system SHALL return all revisions and resolution records in per-visit committed transaction sequence without omitting superseded or non-winning revisions.
+- [ ] **REC-020**: The record store SHALL assign per-visit history sequence numbers deterministically so that equal server timestamps do not make history order ambiguous.
+- [ ] **REC-021**: When a caller retrieves current state or history, the system SHALL not resolve, add to, remove from, or otherwise mutate the visit's outstanding review set.
+
+## Resolution
+
+- [ ] **REC-006**: When a coordinator resolves review work, the system SHALL retain the coordinator's identity, server resolution time, selected revision ID, and every contributing revision ID in receipt order with the resolution outcome.
+- [ ] **REC-022**: When an authenticated user who is not a coordinator attempts to resolve a review set, the system SHALL reject the request without changing visit state or recording a resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-023**: When a coordinator selects a revision from the outstanding review set and supplies that exact complete expected set, the system SHALL atomically set the selected complete revision as current and clear the outstanding review set without modifying its payload or creating a scientific revision.
+- [ ] **REC-024**: If the outstanding review set differs from the expected set supplied for resolution, then the system SHALL reject the resolution with a stale-review-set error without changing visit state or recording a resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-025**: If the selected revision is not in the current outstanding review set, then the system SHALL reject the resolution without changing visit state or recording a resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-026**: If another revision joins an outstanding review set before resolution commits, the system SHALL serialize the resolution against that submission and reject the resolution when its expected set no longer matches.
+- [ ] **REC-027**: When an expected review set is evaluated, the system SHALL compare revision IDs independently of array order and reject a set containing duplicate revision IDs before comparing it with the outstanding set.
+- [ ] **REC-028**: If a resolution request has both a stale expected set and a selected revision outside the outstanding set, then the system SHALL return the stale-review-set error.
+- [ ] **REC-029**: When the same coordinator repeats an accepted resolution with the same visit ID, selected revision ID, and expected revision set, the system SHALL return the original resolution record without changing visit state or recording another resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-030**: When a revision becomes current through resolution, the system SHALL use that revision's ID as the base against which subsequent ordinary concurrency acceptance is evaluated.
+
+## Errors and Provenance
+
+- [ ] **REC-031**: For every rejected submission, retrieval, or resolution request, the system SHALL return a stable machine-readable error code identifying the applicable access, input, resource, idempotency, review-state, or selection failure without including another user's submission payload in the error.
+- [ ] **REC-032**: The record store SHALL retain provenance at whole-visit revision granularity and SHALL NOT automatically combine fields from independently submitted revisions.
